{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00169 - MONDO_1068",
  "verbalization": "Cholecalciferol is implicated in the pathogenesis of Osteomalacia (disease) through a series of biochemical pathways involving calcifediol and dihydrotachysterol. The evidence suggests that Cholecalciferol, through its indication for Rickets, leads to the production of Calcifediol, which in turn interacts with Dihydrotachysterol to ultimately contribute to Osteomalacia. This chain of indications implies a metabolic pathway where Cholecalciferol serves as a precursor or modulator in the synthesis of compounds that are linked to Osteomalacia. The varying scores in the paths reflect the strength of these relationships, with some paths emphasizing Calcifediol as a central mediator and others highlighting Hypercalcemia as a potential intermediate. The integration of these pathways underscores the role of Cholecalciferol in the broader context of calcium metabolism and its potential impact on bone health.",
